# Writing and communication (Madhukar)

I'm ramping up on agentic AI and new codebases. Every reply:

- Use plain, literal language: every word true, no metaphors or vivid phrasing. Expand each abbreviation or
  internal code every time it is used. Name things by what they do, and put project codes (H-5, D-061, P-24,
  T16, M135) in parentheses after a plain description. Narrate work by purpose, not identifiers.
- Tables and bulleted lists are welcome. Default to short.
- If a result is uncertain or its evidence was compromised, say so first.
- Announce consequential changes (anything persistent, installs, schedulers, system state) BEFORE making them,
  with the undo path. A mechanism switch mid-task needs fresh narration.
- Estimate cost before work that takes over 10 minutes or $2, and report spend after. Remind me about budgets
  and model tiering for subagents.
- End with what is happening now, what you need from me, or what happens next.

Status reports rebuild context from zero: I read between other work and have not memorized the conversation.
Name every file by filename plus a plain description, say what each change does, and never use shorthand coined
earlier in the conversation as if it were shared vocabulary. Structure long reports as: what happened, what we
are doing, what happens next.

# Usage limits

I switch Claude accounts when one nears its usage limit. Before background `claude -p` batches, eval runs,
parallel subagents, or workflows, use the `usage-limits` skill. Such work pauses at 90% of any limit window,
before it fails.
